"<p>Every marketplace app, standalone database, and custom compose app can be backed up manually or on a recurring schedule, then restored in place or cloned as a brand-new app.</p>\n<h2>What an App Backup Contains</h2>\n<p>App backups are built on the app's server by an isolated throwaway tools container (<code>nitrobase-backup-tools</code>, no network, no <code>MKNOD</code>) as a zstd-compressed archive (<code>.tar.zst</code>, <code>BACKUP_ZSTD_LEVEL=6</code>) and uploaded via <code>rclone</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>manifest.json</code>: App ID, template version, pinned commit SHA, and the SHA-256 checksum and byte size of every archive member.</li>\n<li><code>env.enc</code>: The app's <code>.env</code> and service secrets, encrypted with <code>ENCRYPTION_KEY</code> (AES-256-GCM Format v3).</li>\n<li><code>compose/docker-compose.yml</code>: The deployed compose file.</li>\n<li><code>db/&#x3C;file></code>: Consistent database dump (e.g. <code>pg_dumpall</code>, <code>mysqldump</code>, <code>mariadb-dump</code>, Redis RDB, MongoDB archive) when the template or engine defines a database strategy.</li>\n<li><code>files/&#x3C;n>.tar</code> &#x26; <code>volumes/&#x3C;n>.tar</code>: Configured bind <code>paths</code> and named Docker <code>volumes</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When a template specifies both <code>stop</code> and a database dump, Nitrobase stops writer services first (leaving the database container running), dumps the database consistently, stops the database if configured, archives files/volumes, and restarts only the services that were running before the backup.</p>\n<h2>Schedules, Retention &#x26; Catch-Up</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open an app or database and go to the <strong>Backups</strong> tab (<code>backup.policy.manage</code> permission).</li>\n<li>Select a <strong>Destination</strong>, <strong>Cron schedule</strong> (default <code>0 3 * * *</code>, minimum interval <code>60</code> minutes via <code>BACKUP_MIN_INTERVAL_MINUTES</code>), and <strong>Keep last</strong> retention count (default <code>7</code>, max <code>1000</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Missed backup catch-up (<code>BACKUP_CATCH_UP=true</code>):</strong> If Nitrobase was offline when a scheduled backup was due, it detects the missed run and catches up automatically on startup.</li>\n<li><strong>Stale backup alerts:</strong> Nitrobase checks enabled schedules daily (<code>BACKUP_STALE_CRON</code>, default <code>37 7 * * *</code>) and emits <code>backup.stale</code> if no successful backup occurred within <code>BACKUP_STALE_DAYS</code> (default <code>2</code> days).</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Restoring a Backup</h2>\n<p>Nitrobase verifies the archive's SHA-256 checksum, validates that it contains only regular files and expected members, checks every member against <code>manifest.json</code>, and then restores:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Restore in place:</strong> Redeploys the app at the backed-up commit/version and loads the database dump and volumes before starting services.</li>\n<li><strong>Restore as a new app:</strong> Creates a new app with its own folder and domains loaded with the backed-up data.</li>\n<li><strong>Download archive:</strong> Download <code>.tar.zst</code> archives directly from the dashboard (<code>backup.download</code> permission; for databases, also requires <code>database.credentials.reveal</code>).</li>\n</ul>"